tation of  electrodynamics or the planning Vulcans  did not outlive their usefulness but not  all of these ideas do as Katy Mac has  pointed out it may turn out the quantum  mechanics might just be just tons of  epicycles on top of each other to get  the everything to work out okay what's  all this got to do with Wolfram language  well if the language makes it much  easier to use mathematics to solve  problems than any tool in history before  it I don't know of anyone in my  generation who was involved in science  who did not wish that he or she had  Mathematica in school Wolfram language  is a game changer in the least two broad  areas first Wolfram language is easy  enough to use that people who are not  mathematicians physicists or engineers  might be enticed to use it I grant that  this is not going to be easy given the  way that most K to 12 education has  hidden the beauty of math from 90% of  the population by redefining what math  is just something that is really boring  and then making it difficult to escape  the trap that the students are placed in  however I am optimistic that it will be  possible to interest philosophers and  others in the humanities camp to use  Wolfram language maths has been in both  the CP snows two worlds for most of its  existence and I am certain that many  as exist to make this a reality again  and Stephen Wolfram mentioned this and  his keynote last night when he said that  you know mathematics really is a liberal  art I believe in a lot of other people  believe that we need philosophers to  help build this brave new world simple  computational thinking or mathematical  thinking is only a first step  secondly Wolfram language is the ideal  tool to deal with the issues inherent in  the current music of math particularly  machine learning as more people use  machine learning more people those who  have subject matter expertise not  mathematical knowledge will come to see  the inherent weakness in using a tool  whose inner workings are a black box  people will figure out how to penetrate  this back box at least well enough to  understand the way uncertainty is  handled in any model that uses machine  learning and I think some of the talks  in this conference illustrate this the  primary danger here is that we are so  enamored of our beautiful tools that we  fall victim to what Mickens calls  technological manifest destiny those of  us at this conference may not even  realize its presence much the same way  that fish are not aware of water the key  point is that technology is not neutral  history is littered with the  consequences of forgetting this we can  see the results all over entire fields  of thought are being devalued because  they are being seen as inconsequential  to the STEM fields other fields are  being replaced by changelings that  follow the forms of physics but without  understanding Richard Feynman called it  cargo cult science large numbers of  people rejecting science entirely rather  than being reduced to a number because  there is no connection to their actual  subjective lives Steve Jobs was right  what we need is the marriage of science  and the liberal arts  okay and there's the further reading and  so my rant is done okay and I I really  hope that you look at some of this  further reading the the the Caputo book  in general was what got me off on this  jag I found it in the bookstore in  London and it's really quite quite  interesting  and if any of you wander over into that  section of the bookstore you might find  some other things okay any questions  comments
 

